## Problem

`Utils::sanitizeDeclaration()` (src/PHPDocsMD/Utils.php:51-69) mutates `$p` inside the loop but
writes it back using the **value** as the array key rather than the loop key:

```php
foreach ($parts as $p) {
    if (self::shouldPrefixWithNamespace($p)) {
        $p = self::sanitizeClassName('\\' . trim($currentNameSpace, '\\') . '\\' . $p);
    } elseif (self::isClassReference($p)) {
        $p = self::sanitizeClassName($p);
    }
    $parts[$p] = $p;     // <- should be $parts[$key] = $p
}
```

The original un-prefixed entry is never replaced, so a new element is appended alongside it.
`array_unique` cannot collapse them because the strings now differ. The result is joined with
`' | '` — and a raw `|` inside a markdown table cell terminates the cell, so the generated table
row gains a spurious column.

## Evidence

```
$ php -r '... var_export(PHPDocsMD\Utils::sanitizeDeclaration("Foo", "Acme"));'
'Foo | \\Acme\\Foo'
$ ... sanitizeDeclaration("Foo|Bar", "Acme")
'Foo | Bar | \\Acme\\Foo | \\Acme\\Bar'
```

Expected: `'\Acme\Foo'` and `'\Acme\Foo | \Acme\Bar'`.

End to end, `@return Gadget` in namespace `Demo` renders:

```
| public | <strong>doThing(...)</strong> : <em>Gadget | \Demo\Gadget</em><br />... |
```

That row now has four `|`-delimited cells in a two-column table.

The project's own committed `docs.md` already carries four such broken rows (lines 85, 86, 115,
308), e.g.

```
| public | <strong>find(...)</strong> : <em>[\PHPDocsMD\Entities\FunctionEntity](#...)  | bool</em> |
```

## Impact

Every relative class reference in `@param`/`@return` is emitted twice, once wrong, and any
multi-type declaration corrupts the markdown table it is rendered into. Renderers show a stray
extra column and the type text is wrong.

## Fix

Key by the loop key and escape the join for table safety:

```php
foreach ($parts as $key => $p) {
    if (self::shouldPrefixWithNamespace($p)) {
        $p = self::sanitizeClassName('\\' . trim($currentNameSpace, '\\') . '\\' . $p);
    } elseif (self::isClassReference($p)) {
        $p = self::sanitizeClassName($p);
    }
    $parts[$key] = $p;
}
$parts = array_unique($parts, SORT_STRING);
return implode(' | ', $parts);
```

Separately, `MDTableGenerator::addFunc()` must escape `|` as `\|` (or `&#124;`) in every value it
interpolates into a table cell — type, name, default and description — so a legitimate union type
cannot break the table. Add a test asserting `sanitizeDeclaration('Foo', 'Acme') === '\Acme\Foo'`
and one asserting a union return type renders as a single cell.

## Resolution
sanitizeDeclaration() keys by loop key ($parts[$key] = $p) and array_unique uses SORT_STRING. MDTableGenerator::escapeCell() escapes | as \| in every interpolated cell value. UtilsTest + testPipesAreEscapedInsideTableCells cover both. docs.md rows 60/61/135 now stay in two columns.

## Problem

`MDTableGenerator::addFunc()` (src/PHPDocsMD/MDTableGenerator.php:88) tests the default value for
truthiness:

```php
if ($param->getDefault()) {
    $paramStr .= '=' . $param->getDefault();
}
```

`ParamEntity::getDefault()` returns a `string` (src/PHPDocsMD/Entities/ParamEntity.php:17). PHP
treats the string `'0'` as falsy, so a numeric-zero default is skipped. `Reflector::createParameterEntity()`
(src/PHPDocsMD/Reflections/Reflector.php:330-341) only stringifies string, bool, null and array
defaults; ints and floats fall through unchanged and are coerced to `'0'` on assignment.

## Evidence

```php
/** Zero default */
public function zero($n = 0, $f = 0.0, $s = '') {}
```

renders as

```
| public | <strong>zero(</strong><em>mixed</em> <strong>$n</strong>, <em>mixed</em> <strong>$f</strong>, <em>string</em> <strong>$s=`''`</strong>)</strong> : <em>void</em>...
```

`$n` and `$f` show no default at all; `$s = ''` (also falsy as a value, but wrapped in backticks
so the string is non-empty) survives only by accident.

## Impact

Generated docs claim required parameters that are in fact optional. `0` is one of the most common
default values in real code (offsets, flags, counters).

## Fix

Distinguish "has a default" from "the default is truthy". Give `ParamEntity` an explicit flag set
by the reflector, and test that instead:

```php
// ParamEntity
private bool $hasDefault = false;
public function hasDefault(): bool { return $this->hasDefault; }
public function setDefault(string $default): self
{
    $this->default = $default;
    $this->hasDefault = true;
    return $this;
}

// MDTableGenerator::addFunc()
if ($param->hasDefault()) {
    $paramStr .= '=' . $param->getDefault();
}
```

and in `Reflector::createParameterEntity()` route through `$reflection->isDefaultValueAvailable()`
rather than catching an exception, formatting int/float via `var_export($def, true)`. Add a test
covering `$n = 0`, `$f = 0.0` and `$b = false`.

## Resolution
ParamEntity tracks hasDefault() separately from the value; Reflector uses isDefaultValueAvailable() and var_export for int/float. MDTableGenerator tests hasDefault() instead of truthiness. Covered by testFalsyDefaultsAreRetained (0, 0.0, false, null).

## Problem

bin/phpdoc-md:18-24:

```php
try {
    $cli = new CLI();
    $code = $cli->run();
    exit($code);
} catch (Exception $e) {
    exit(2);
}
```

`$e` is captured and discarded. Anything thrown before Symfony's own error rendering takes over —
notably `CLI::__construct()`'s `json_decode(..., JSON_THROW_ON_ERROR)` on a missing or malformed
`composer.json` (src/PHPDocsMD/Console/CLI.php:21-26) — exits 2 with zero output.

The autoloader probe above it has the same shape (bin/phpdoc-md:9-14): if none of the three
candidate paths exists the loop simply ends, and the next line dies with an unhandled
`Error: Class "PHPDocsMD\Console\CLI" not found` — which the `catch (Exception)` does not catch,
since `Error` does not extend `Exception`.

## Evidence

`file_get_contents()` returns `false` for a missing file; `json_decode(false, ...)` with
`JSON_THROW_ON_ERROR` throws `JsonException`, which is an `Exception`. The catch block therefore
converts a diagnosable configuration problem into a bare exit code with an empty stderr.

## Impact

Users and CI see "exit 2" with no explanation. There is no way to tell a missing `composer.json`
from a malformed one, and no stack trace to work from.

## Fix

Fail loudly on a missing autoloader, and report what actually went wrong:

```php
$autoLoadPaths = [...];
$loaded = false;
foreach ($autoLoadPaths as $autoloader) {
    if (file_exists($autoloader)) {
        require $autoloader;
        $loaded = true;
        break;
    }
}
if (!$loaded) {
    fwrite(STDERR, "phpdoc-md: no composer autoloader found. Run 'composer install'.\n");
    exit(2);
}

use PHPDocsMD\Console\CLI;

try {
    exit((new CLI())->run());
} catch (Throwable $e) {
    fwrite(STDERR, 'phpdoc-md: ' . $e->getMessage() . PHP_EOL);
    exit(2);
}
```

`Throwable` also covers the `Error` case the current catch misses.

## Resolution
bin/phpdoc-md now reports a missing autoloader on stderr and exits 2, and catches Throwable (not Exception) printing the message. Error is now covered too.

## Problem

`UseInspector::getUseStatementsInString()` is a naive substring split over the entire file:

```php
$chunks = array_slice(preg_split('/use[\s+]/', $content), 1);
foreach ($chunks as $chunk) {
    $usage = trim(current(explode(';', $chunk)));
    $usages[] = Utils::sanitizeClassName($usage);
}
```

It has no notion of statement position or syntax. Consequences:

1. Any `use` followed by whitespace anywhere splits — inside `because `, inside a closure's
   `use ($n)`, inside comment prose.
2. `use Foo\Bar as Baz;` yields the literal `\Foo\Bar as Baz`.
3. `use Foo\{Bar, Baz};` yields `\Foo\{Bar, Baz}`.
4. `use function count;` yields `\function count`.

`Reflector::getReturnTypesArray()` matches a docblock return type against this list by base name
(`stripAwayNamespace()`), so aliases and group imports never match and the type falls through to
`sanitizeDeclaration()`, which prefixes it with the *current* namespace.

## Evidence

Garbage entries, on a file with a closure and the word "because":

```
$ php -r '... (new UseInspector)->getUseStatementsInFile("Src/Because.php")'
array(
  0 => '\Probe\Aye',
  1 => '\and a closure use.\n */\nclass Beca',
  2 => '\{\n    public function run(array $xs): array\n    {\n        $n = 1',
  3 => '\reasons\n        return array_map(function ($x)',
  4 => '\($n) { return $x + $n',
)
```

The same on this repository's own `src/PHPDocsMD/Reflections/Reflector.php`:

```
  15 => '\function count',
  16 => '\slash instead of pipe or md-generation will get it wrong\n        $def = false',
```

Wrong output, end to end:

```php
namespace Probe2;
use Probe2\Vendor\LongName as Short;
use Probe2\Vendor\{Alpha};

class Aliased {
    /** @return Short */  public function aliased() {}
    /** @return Alpha */  public function grouped() {}
}
```

```
| public | <strong>aliased()</strong> : <em>\Probe2\Short</em> |
| public | <strong>grouped()</strong> : <em>\Probe2\Alpha</em> |
```

Both are wrong. `\Probe2\Short` and `\Probe2\Alpha` do not exist; the correct answers are
`\Probe2\Vendor\LongName` and `\Probe2\Vendor\Alpha`, and both would have been rendered as
cross-reference links had they resolved.

## Impact

Aliased imports and grouped imports are both common. Every `@return`/`@param` written against an
alias or a grouped import resolves to a non-existent class in the current namespace, and loses its
cross-reference link. The garbage entries are a standing collision risk: any junk chunk whose base
name happens to equal a sought type wins the `getReturnTypesArray()` loop before the real import.

## Fix

Tokenise instead of string-splitting. `token_get_all()` is in core, needs no dependency, and
resolves all four cases:

```php
public function getUseStatementsInString(string $content): array
{
    $usages = [];
    $tokens = token_get_all($content);
    for ($i = 0, $n = count($tokens); $i < $n; $i++) {
        if (!is_array($tokens[$i]) || $tokens[$i][0] !== T_USE) {
            continue;
        }
        // A closure's `use` is preceded by ')' — skip it.
        for ($j = $i - 1; $j >= 0 && is_array($tokens[$j]) && $tokens[$j][0] === T_WHITESPACE; $j--);
        if (($tokens[$j] ?? null) === ')') {
            continue;
        }
        // collect until ';' or '{', tracking `as` aliases and group bodies
        ...
    }
    return $usages;
}
```

`UseInspectorTest::testInspection` currently asserts only the well-formed cases; add cases for
`use A\B as C;`, `use A\{B, C};`, `use function strlen;`, a closure `use ($x)`, and prose
containing "because".

## Resolution
UseInspector rewritten on token_get_all(). Prose, closure captures and comment text no longer split; 'use function'/'use const' skipped; group imports expanded; the result is now keyed by the local name (alias or last segment) so aliases resolve. Reflector::getReturnTypesArray() looks up by that key. Regressions: 6 UseInspectorTest cases plus ReflectorTest::testAliasedAndGroupedImportsResolve.

## Problem

`DocInfoExtractor::extractInfoFromComment()` decides whether a docblock line opens a
tag with a substring test (DocInfoExtractor.php:61):

```php
if (!str_contains($words[0], '@')) {
    // Append to tag
```

`{@inheritDoc}` therefore takes the tag branch, and `substr($words[0], 1)` strips only
the leading `{`, producing a tag named `@inheritDoc}`. `DocInfo::shouldInheritDoc()`
(DocInfo.php:66) looks for the keys `inheritDoc` / `inheritdoc`, neither of which is
set, so `Reflector::createFunctionEntity()` never takes the
`findInheritedFunctionDeclaration()` path.

The braced inline form is the spelling phpDocumentor documents and the one PhpStorm
inserts by default; the bare `@inheritDoc` form works correctly.

## Evidence

```php
abstract class Base {
    /**
     * Documented on the parent.
     * @param int $n a number
     * @return string
     */
    abstract public function work(int $n): string;
}

class BareChild extends Base {
    /** @inheritDoc */
    public function work(int $n): string { return ''; }
}

class BracedChild extends Base {
    /** {@inheritDoc} */
    public function work(int $n): string { return ''; }
}
```

Generated output:

```
### Class: \Probe\Inh\BareChild
| public | <strong>work(</strong><em>int</em> <strong>$n</strong>)</strong> : <em>string</em><br /><em>Documented on the parent.</em> |

### Class: \Probe\Inh\BracedChild
| public | <strong>work(</strong><em>int</em> <strong>$n</strong>)</strong> : <em>string</em> |
```

`BracedChild::work` renders with no description. `BareChild::work` inherits it.

## Impact

Every method documented with the standard braced form loses its inherited
description, parameter descriptions and `@see` entries in the generated document —
silently, since the row still renders. Codebases that follow phpDocumentor's own
documentation, or that were written in PhpStorm, lose inherited docs across the board.

## Fix

Recognise the tag word by shape rather than by substring, accepting the optional
braces. In `extractInfoFromComment()`:

```php
// A tag is the first word, optionally wrapped in the inline braces
// phpDocumentor uses: "@param", "{@inheritDoc}". A word that merely
// contains "@" (an e-mail address) is prose, not a tag.
$isTag = preg_match('/^\{?@([A-Za-z][\w-]*)\}?$/', $words[0], $tagMatch) === 1;

if (!$isTag) {
    $joinWith = $current_tag === 'example' ? PHP_EOL : ' ';
    $tags[$current_tag] .= $joinWith . $line;
} elseif ($tagMatch[1] === 'param') {
    ...
} elseif ($tagMatch[1] === 'see') {
    ...
} else {
    $current_tag = $tagMatch[1];
    ...
}
```

Use `$tagMatch[1]` in place of both `$words[0] === '@param'` / `'@see'` comparisons
and the `substr($words[0], 1)` in the else branch.

The same change also fixes the description-truncation defect filed separately for
`@`-containing prose words.

Add a fixture with `{@inheritDoc}` and assert its description equals the parent's.

## Resolution
Tag detection is now a shape match accepting the inline braces. Also made a missing parent non-fatal (findInheritedFunctionDeclaration returns false, caller falls through) rather than aborting the document. Test: testBracedInheritDocInheritsTheParentDescription.

## Problem

`Reflector::createParameterEntity()` lines 329-347 contain two ternaries whose
condition is decided by the branch they sit in, so one arm of each can never run:

```php
if ($docs['type']) {                                    // <- truthy
    $posClassA = Utils::getClassBaseName($docs['type']);
    $posClassB = Utils::getClassBaseName($declaredType);
    if ($posClassA === $posClassB) {
        $docs['type'] = $declaredType;
    } else {
        $docs['type'] = empty($docs['type'])            // always false here
            ? $declaredType
            : $docs['type'] . '|' . $declaredType;
    }
} else {                                                // <- falsy
    $docs['type'] = empty($docs['type'])                // always true here
        ? $declaredType
        : $docs['type'] . '|' . $declaredType;
}
```

In the `if` arm `$docs['type']` is truthy, so `empty($docs['type'])` is false and the
`$declaredType` arm is unreachable. In the `else` arm `$docs['type']` is falsy, and
the only falsy string `empty()` disagrees with is `'0'` — not a legal type name — so
`empty()` is true and the concatenation arm is unreachable.

Both dead ternaries collapse to a constant.

## Evidence

Reduced by substituting the branch condition into each ternary:

- `if` arm: `$docs['type']` truthy ⇒ `empty($docs['type']) === false` ⇒ the ternary
  always yields `$docs['type'] . '|' . $declaredType`.
- `else` arm: `$docs['type']` falsy and never `'0'` ⇒ `empty($docs['type']) === true`
  ⇒ the ternary always yields `$declaredType`.

The suite is unchanged by the simplification below — `vendor/bin/phpunit` stays at
86 tests / 253 assertions, and `composer docs-check` produces no diff.

## Impact

Maintenance only, but this is the single hardest-to-read block in the codebase: a
reader has to prove the unreachability themselves before they can reason about the
type-merging rules, and the duplicated concatenation reads as though the two arms
differ when they do not.

## Fix

```php
if ($declaredType && $declaredType !== $docs['type'] &&
    !($declaredType === 'array' && str_ends_with($docs['type'], '[]'))
) {
    if ($docs['type'] === '') {
        $docs['type'] = $declaredType;
    } elseif (Utils::getClassBaseName($docs['type']) === Utils::getClassBaseName($declaredType)) {
        // Same class, one written short and one fully qualified — prefer the
        // declared type, which is already resolved.
        $docs['type'] = $declaredType;
    } else {
        $docs['type'] = $docs['type'] . '|' . $declaredType;
    }
}
```

Three arms, each reachable, same behaviour. Verify with `vendor/bin/phpunit` and
`composer docs-check`.

## Resolution
Collapsed to three reachable branches. Suite and docs-check unchanged, confirming behaviour is identical.

## Problem

`DocInfoExtractor::extractInfoFromComment()` sends every unrecognised tag through one
branch that stores the rest of the line verbatim:

```php
} else {
    // Start new tag
    $current_tag = $tagName;
    array_splice($words, 0, 1);
    if (empty($tags[$current_tag])) {
        $tags[$current_tag] = '';
    }

    $tags[$current_tag] .= trim(implode(' ', $words));
}
```

That is right for `@deprecated` and `@example`, whose value *is* the rest of the line. It is
wrong for `@return`, whose documented form is `@return <type> <description>` — only the
first word is the type.

`DocInfo::getReturnType()` hands the whole string to `Reflector::getReturnType()`, which
renders it as the return type whenever the method has no declared one.

## Evidence

```php
class Wrap
{
    /**
     * Returns a thing.
     *
     * @return string the rendered name
     */
    public function returnsWithDescription() {}

    /**
     * Returns a thing with a declared type.
     *
     * @return string the rendered name
     */
    public function returnsTyped(): string { return ''; }
}
```

```
$ ./bin/phpdoc-md generate --bootstrap=boot.php 'Probe\Wrap'
| public | <strong>returnsWithDescription()</strong> : <em>string the rendered name</em><br /><em>Returns a thing.</em> |
| public | <strong>returnsTyped()</strong> : <em>string</em><br /><em>Returns a thing with a declared type.</em> |
```

The parsed value is the whole line:

```
return type: 'string the rendered name'
```

`returnsTyped()` is correct only by accident — its declared `: string` overrides the
docblock, which is the path `Reflector::getReturnType()` takes when `hasReturnType()` is
true. Remove the declared type and the same docblock produces the same corruption.

## Impact

`@return <type> <description>` is the phpDocumentor-documented form and the shape every
IDE inserts. A codebase that has not yet added declared return types — the exact codebase
that most needs a docblock-driven documentation generator — gets a return-type column full
of English sentences.

It also poisons everything downstream that treats the value as a type:
`Utils::isClassReference()` rejects it because it contains spaces, so no cross-reference
link is produced and no namespace resolution is attempted.

## Fix

Take only the type from `@return`, and keep continuation lines out of it:

```php
} elseif ($tagName === 'return') {
    // "@return <type> <description>" — only the first word is the type.
    // Storing the whole line rendered the description inside <em> as if it
    // were part of the type.
    $tags['return'] = $words[1] ?? '';
    $current_tag = 'return';
} else {
    // Start new tag
    ...
}
```

and in the non-tag branch, treat `return` like `param` — its wrapped lines are prose, not
type, and are not rendered anywhere:

```php
if (in_array($current_tag, ['param', 'return'], true)) {
    ...
    continue;
}
```

Add to `ReflectorTest` a fixture method with `@return string the rendered name` and **no**
declared return type, asserting the rendered type is exactly `string`. Verify
`composer docs-check` and regenerate `docs.md` with `composer docs` if it changes.

## Resolution
@return now takes only the first word as the type, in its own branch. Test: testOnlyTheFirstWordOfReturnIsTheType.

## Problem

`DocInfoExtractor::applyInfoToEntity()` decides that something is deprecated by testing the
deprecation *message*, not the presence of the tag:

```php
if ($docInfo->getDeprecationMessage()) {
    $code->isDeprecated(true);
    $code->setDeprecationMessage($docInfo->getDeprecationMessage());
}
```

`@deprecated` on its own — no version, no replacement, no explanation — parses to an empty
string, which is falsy, so the entity is never marked deprecated. The bare form is the
form the tag is most often written in.

## Evidence

```php
class Edge
{
    /**
     * @deprecated
     */
    public function deprecatedNoMessage() {}

    /**
     * @deprecated since 2.0, use Other instead
     */
    public function deprecatedWithMessage() {}
}
```

```
$ ./bin/phpdoc-md generate --bootstrap=boot.php 'Probe\Edge'
| public | <strong>deprecatedNoMessage()</strong> : <em>void</em> |
| public | <del><strong>deprecatedWithMessage()</strong> : <em>void</em></del><br /><em>DEPRECATED - since 2.0, use Other instead</em> |
```

The first row is indistinguishable from a method that carries no `@deprecated` at all: no
strikethrough, no notice. The second is rendered correctly, which is what makes the first a
silent omission rather than an unimplemented feature.

The same applies to classes — `ClassEntity::isDeprecated()` drives the `<del>` heading and
the `> **DEPRECATED**` block in `PHPDocsMDCommand::execute()`, and both are skipped for a
bare `@deprecated` on a class.

## Impact

Deprecation is the one piece of information a reader most needs to see before using an API,
and the strikethrough is the only visual signal the generated document offers. A codebase
that marks deprecations without prose — which is valid, and common where the replacement is
obvious — gets a document that says nothing is deprecated.

## Fix

Record whether the tag was present, separately from its text. In `DocInfo`:

```php
private array $data;
private bool $hasDeprecatedTag;

public function __construct(array $data = [])
{
    // $data holds only the tags actually seen; $defaultStructure supplies the
    // rest, so "was the tag written?" has to be answered before the merge.
    $this->hasDeprecatedTag = array_key_exists('deprecated', $data);
    $this->data = array_merge(self::$defaultStructure, $data);
}

public function isDeprecated(): bool
{
    return $this->hasDeprecatedTag;
}
```

In `DocInfoExtractor::applyInfoToEntity()`:

```php
if ($docInfo->isDeprecated()) {
    $code->isDeprecated(true);
    $code->setDeprecationMessage($docInfo->getDeprecationMessage());
}
```

`MDTableGenerator::addFunc()` then needs to not emit a dangling separator for the empty
message:

```php
if ($func->isDeprecated()) {
    $str = '<del>' . $str . '</del>';
    $str .= $func->getDeprecationMessage() === ''
        ? '<br /><em>DEPRECATED</em>'
        : '<br /><em>DEPRECATED - ' . self::escapeCell($func->getDeprecationMessage()) . '</em>';
}
```

and `PHPDocsMDCommand::execute()` likewise for the class-level `> **DEPRECATED** ` line,
which would otherwise render as `> **DEPRECATED** ` with a trailing space.

Add to `ReflectorTest` and `PHPDocsMDCommandTest` cases for a bare `@deprecated` on a method
and on a class, asserting the strikethrough and notice are present and carry no trailing
separator.

## Resolution
DocInfo records tag presence (array_key_exists before the defaults merge) and exposes isDeprecated(); applyInfoToEntity() tests that instead of the message. MDTableGenerator and the class heading emit DEPRECATED with no dangling separator when the message is empty. Tests: testDeprecatedWithoutAMessageIsStillDeprecated, testDeprecationWithoutAMessageHasNoDanglingSeparator.

## Problem

`DocInfoExtractor::extractInfoFromComment()` tracks which tag subsequent lines belong to in
`$current_tag`, and every branch that starts a tag updates it — except the `@param` branch:

```php
if (!$isTag) {
    // Append to tag
    $joinWith = $current_tag === 'example' ? PHP_EOL : ' ';
    $tags[$current_tag] .= $joinWith . $line;
} elseif ($tagName === 'param') {
    // Get parameter declaration
    if ($paramData = $this->figureOutParamDeclaration($words, $currentNamespace)) {
        [$name, $data] = $paramData;
        $tags['params'][$name] = $data;
    }
} elseif ($tagName === 'see') {
    ...
} else {
    // Start new tag
    $current_tag = $tagName;
    ...
}
```

`@param` writes into `$tags['params']` and leaves `$current_tag` pointing at whatever it was
before — for the overwhelmingly common layout, `description`. The continuation of a wrapped
`@param` is not a tag, so it takes the first branch and is concatenated onto the method's
description.

`@see` has the same gap.

## Evidence

```php
class Wrap
{
    /**
     * Does a thing.
     *
     * @param string $name The name of the thing, which needs
     *                     quite a long explanation to describe.
     * @param int    $n    A short one.
     */
    public function wrapped(string $name, int $n) {}
}
```

```
$ ./bin/phpdoc-md generate --bootstrap=boot.php 'Probe\Wrap'
| public | <strong>wrapped(</strong><em>string</em> <strong>$name</strong>, <em>int</em> <strong>$n</strong>)</strong> : <em>void</em><br /><em>Does a thing. quite a long explanation to describe.</em> |
```

The rendered description is `Does a thing. quite a long explanation to describe.` — a
sentence fragment lifted out of a parameter and glued onto the method summary. Reading the
parsed structure confirms the split:

```
description: 'Does a thing. quite a long explanation to describe.'
param $name: array(
  'description' => 'The name of the thing, which needs',   <- truncated at the wrap
  ...
)
```

Found in the wild the same way, in `vimeo/psalm`, where the wrapped line is a *type*
continuation:

```php
/**
 * @param  class-string<MethodVisibilityProviderInterface>
 *     |class-string<MethodVisibilityProviderInterface> $class
 */
```

```
| public | <strong>registerClass(</strong><em>string</em> <strong>$class</strong>) …
  <br /><em>\|class-string<MethodVisibilityProviderInterface> $class</em> |
```

The method's rendered description is a fragment of a type expression.

## Impact

Multi-line `@param` descriptions are the normal way to document a parameter that needs more
than half a line, and every wrapped line lands in the wrong field: truncated off the
parameter it belongs to, and appended to a method summary it has nothing to do with. The
result reads as a garbled sentence in the one column of the table a reader scans first.

## Fix

Route continuation lines to the parameter they continue:

```php
$tags = [$current_tag => ''];
$lastParam = null;

foreach (explode(PHP_EOL, $comment) as $line) {
    ...
    if (!$isTag) {
        if ($current_tag === 'param') {
            // A wrapped @param line continues that parameter's description.
            // Without this it falls through and is appended to the method
            // description instead.
            if ($lastParam !== null) {
                $tags['params'][$lastParam]['description'] = trim(
                    $tags['params'][$lastParam]['description'] . ' ' . $line
                );
            }
            continue;
        }

        $joinWith = $current_tag === 'example' ? PHP_EOL : ' ';
        $tags[$current_tag] .= $joinWith . $line;
    } elseif ($tagName === 'param') {
        $lastParam = null;
        if ($paramData = $this->figureOutParamDeclaration($words, $currentNamespace)) {
            [$name, $data] = $paramData;
            $tags['params'][$name] = $data;
            $lastParam = $name;
        }
        $current_tag = 'param';
    } elseif ($tagName === 'see') {
        ...
        $current_tag = 'see';
    } else {
        ...
    }
}
```

`$current_tag = 'param'` is what stops the fall-through; the `continue` is what keeps
`$tags['param']` — a key nothing ever reads — from being created. `$lastParam = null` on a
malformed `@param` makes its continuation lines dropped rather than misfiled.

`@see` needs `$current_tag = 'see'` for the same reason, and the non-tag branch must skip it
too, since `$tags['see']` is an array and `.=` on it would be a type error.

Add to `ReflectorTest` (via a `Docblocks` fixture) a method with a wrapped `@param`
description, asserting the method description is exactly the summary and the parameter's
description is the full wrapped text. Verify `composer docs-check` — this repository's own
docblocks contain wrapped `@param` lines, so `docs.md` will change; regenerate it with
`composer docs`.

## Resolution
$current_tag is now set for param/see/return, and a wrapped @param line is appended to that parameter's description instead of the method's. Test: testAWrappedParamLineStaysWithItsParameter.

## Problem

`MDTableGenerator::formatExampleComment()` removes the docblock's leading indentation by
testing three fixed widths and stripping whichever one it finds first:

```php
if (preg_match('/(\n {7})/', $example)) {
    $example = preg_replace('/(\n {7})/', "\n", $example);
} elseif (preg_match('/(\n {4})/', $example)) {
    $example = preg_replace('/(\n {4})/', "\n", $example);
} else {
    $example = preg_replace('/(\n {3})/', "\n", $example);
}
```

`preg_match` searches the *whole* example, so the width chosen is whatever the most deeply
indented line happens to have. `preg_replace` then removes that many spaces from every line
that has them and leaves shorter lines untouched.

The result is not a dedent. Lines nested inside a block lose more indentation than the block
itself, so the nesting inverts.

## Evidence

```php
/**
 * Example with a nested block.
 *
 * @example
 * <code>
 * foreach ($items as $item) {
 *     if ($item->ok()) {
 *         echo $item->name();
 *     }
 * }
 * </code>
 */
class Indent {}
```

Source indentation, relative: `0 / 4 / 8 / 4 / 0`. Rendered:

```
foreach ($items as $item) {
    if ($item->ok()) {
 echo $item->name();
    }
}
```

`0 / 4 / 1 / 4 / 0`. The `echo` — the innermost statement — is rendered *less* indented than
the `if` that contains it.

This is in the repository's own published `docs.md`, from `MDTableGenerator`'s class-level
example:

```php
<?php
     $generator = new PHPDocsMD\MDTableGenerator();
     $generator->openTable();
     foreach($classEntity->getFunctions() as $func) {
      $generator->addFunc( $func );
     }
     echo $generator->getTable();
```

The statement inside the `foreach` sits one space deeper than the `foreach`, where the
source has it a full level deeper.

## Impact

Every `@example` containing a nested block — a loop, a conditional, a try/catch, an array
literal — renders with its structure destroyed. The block is fenced as `php`, so readers
take it as copyable code, and mis-indented code is what they copy. The tool's own
documentation demonstrates the failure.

## Fix

Strip the indentation the lines actually share, rather than guessing a width:

```php
$example = self::stripCodeTags($example);
$example = self::dedent($example);
```

```php
/**
 * Remove the indentation every non-blank line shares.
 *
 * The width has to be the minimum across the example, not the maximum: the old
 * heuristic matched the deepest line's indentation and stripped that many
 * spaces from every line that had them, which left a nested block rendered
 * shallower than the block containing it.
 */
private static function dedent(string $text): string
{
    $indent = null;
    foreach (preg_split('/\R/', $text) ?: [] as $line) {
        if (trim($line) === '') {
            continue;
        }
        $width = strlen($line) - strlen(ltrim($line, ' '));
        $indent = $indent === null ? $width : min($indent, $width);
    }

    return $indent ? (string)preg_replace('/^ {' . $indent . '}/m', '', $text) : $text;
}
```

Blank lines are skipped when measuring and left alone when stripping, so a blank line inside
the example cannot force the width to zero.

This changes `docs.md`, so regenerate it with `composer docs` in the same commit. Add to
`MDTableGeneratorTest` a case whose example has `0 / 4 / 8` relative indentation, asserting
the rendered block preserves those relative widths.

## Resolution
Replaced the 7/4/3-space heuristic with dedent(), which strips the indentation every non-blank line shares. docs.md regenerated: the nested statement in MDTableGenerator's own example is now a full level deeper rather than one space. Test: testNestedIndentationInAnExampleIsPreserved.

## Problem

`Reflector::createParameterEntity()` decides whether the docblock type and the declared
type say the same thing by comparing the two declarations as whole strings:

```php
if ($declaredType && $declaredType !== $docs['type'] &&
    !($declaredType === 'array' && str_ends_with($docs['type'], '[]'))
) {
    if ($docs['type'] === '') {
        $docs['type'] = $declaredType;
    } elseif (Utils::getClassBaseName($docs['type']) === Utils::getClassBaseName($declaredType)) {
        $docs['type'] = $declaredType;
    } else {
        $docs['type'] .= '|' . $declaredType;
    }
}
```

The two sides are produced by different code with different formatting conventions:

- `$docs['type']` comes from `Utils::sanitizeDeclaration()`, which joins members with
  `' | '` (spaces).
- `$declaredType` comes from `Reflector::formatReflectionType()`, which joins with `'|'`
  (no spaces).

So `int | string` and `int|string` — the same type — never compare equal. The
`getClassBaseName()` shortcut does not rescue it either: that helper splits on `\` and
returns the last segment, so it yields `string` for one and `string|int`-shaped fragments
for the other whenever a union is involved.

Every parameter that carries both a `@param` and a declared type therefore falls into the
`.=` arm and gets each member listed twice.

## Evidence

```php
namespace Probe;

class Thing {}

class Dup
{
    /** @param Thing|null $a documented to match the signature exactly */
    public function documented(?Thing $a = null) {}

    public function undocumented(?Thing $a = null) {}

    /** @param int|string $b */
    public function scalarUnion(int|string $b) {}
}
```

```
$ ./bin/phpdoc-md generate --bootstrap=boot.php 'Probe\Dup'
| public | <strong>documented(</strong><em>\Probe\Thing \| null \| \Probe\Thing \| null</em> <strong>$a=null</strong>) …
| public | <strong>scalarUnion(</strong><em>int \| string \| string \| int</em> <strong>$b</strong>) …
| public | <strong>undocumented(</strong><em>\Probe\Thing \| null</em> <strong>$a=null</strong>) …
```

`undocumented()` — identical signature, no docblock — is correct. Adding a `@param` that
*agrees with the signature* is what corrupts the output.

Measured over documentation generated for eleven installed packages (symfony/console,
symfony/string, symfony/finder, symfony/process, phpunit/phpunit, vimeo/psalm,
sebastian/diff, nikic/php-parser, webmozart/assert, psr/log, composer/semver):

```
union type cells: 3708, containing a repeated member: 881
  nikic_php-parser.md:381  callable \| null \| callable \| null
  nikic_php-parser.md:435  \PhpParser\ErrorHandler \| null \| \PhpParser\ErrorHandler \| null
  nikic_php-parser.md:465  \PhpParser\Name \| null \| \PhpParser\Node\Name \| null
  nikic_php-parser.md:527  string \| null \| string \| null
```

**24% of every union type the tool renders repeats at least one member.**

## Impact

The most carefully documented code produces the worst output: a parameter with no docblock
renders correctly, and one whose docblock exactly matches the signature renders
`callable | null | callable | null`. Union and nullable parameters are ordinary in modern
PHP, so this is the single most visible defect in a generated document, and it makes the
tool look broken to anyone reading its output.

## Fix

Merge the two declarations member by member instead of concatenating them:

```php
} else {
    // Both sides may be unions, written with different spacing:
    // sanitizeDeclaration() joins with " | ", the reflection API with "|".
    // Concatenating the two declarations made them read as disjoint and listed
    // every member twice.
    $docs['type'] = self::mergeTypeDeclarations($docs['type'], $declaredType);
}
```

```php
/**
 * Union members from every given declaration, in order, without repeats.
 * Members are compared trimmed and case-insensitively, because the two
 * producers of a type declaration in this class do not agree on spacing.
 */
private static function mergeTypeDeclarations(string ...$declarations): string
{
    $members = [];

    foreach ($declarations as $declaration) {
        foreach (explode('|', $declaration) as $member) {
            $member = trim($member);
            if ($member !== '') {
                $members[strtolower($member)] += 0; // placeholder, see below
                $members[strtolower($member)] = $member;
            }
        }
    }

    return implode('|', $members);
}
```

(drop the placeholder line — written out here only to show the keying; the body is the
`foreach` with `$members[strtolower($member)] = $member;`.)

Keying by the lowercased member is what collapses `int | string` against `int|string`;
`implode('|', …)` re-emits the canonical separator that `ParamEntity::setType()`'s
`preg_replace('/\s*\|\s*/', ' | ', …)` then re-spaces, so the rendered form is unchanged
for types that were already correct.

This also fixes the partial-overlap cases — `\PhpParser\Name | null` merged with
`\PhpParser\Node\Name|null` yields `\PhpParser\Name|null|\PhpParser\Node\Name`, still
reporting the genuine disagreement between docblock and signature but no longer repeating
`null`.

Add to `ReflectorTest` (against `test/Acme/Fixtures/Types/Shapes.php`) cases asserting that
a `@param` matching a nullable class type, a `@param` matching a scalar union, and a
docblock-only type each produce a type string whose `|`-separated members are unique.
Verify with `composer docs-check`: `docs.md` changes, because `DocInfoExtractor` and
`Reflector` both take union parameters, so regenerate it with `composer docs`.

## Resolution
Added Reflector::mergeTypeDeclarations(), which merges union members trimmed and case-insensitively instead of concatenating declarations. Corpus unions repeating a member 1116 -> 0 (the 3 remaining scanner hits are description cells containing ASCII tables and '||' operators, not type cells). Test: testADocumentedUnionIsMergedRatherThanConcatenated.

## Problem

`UseInspector::getUseStatementsInString()` treats every `T_USE` token that is not a closure
capture as an import statement:

```php
if (!is_array($tokens[$i]) || $tokens[$i][0] !== T_USE) {
    continue;
}
if ($this->isClosureCapture($tokens, $i)) {
    continue;
}
foreach ($this->readImport($tokens, $i) as $localName => $import) {
    $usages[$localName] = Utils::sanitizeClassName($import);
}
```

`isClosureCapture()` only rejects the one `use` preceded by `)`. A trait import inside a
class body — `class Foo { use Bar; }` — is not preceded by `)`, so it is read as though it
were a file-level import and written into the map as `\Bar`, unqualified.

The class body is parsed *after* the file header, so when the trait was itself imported at
the top of the file the later, wrong entry overwrites the correct one.

## Evidence

Unit level:

```php
$src = '<?php
namespace Acme;
use Vendor\Thing;
class Foo { use Thing; }';

(new UseInspector)->getUseStatementsInString($src);
// array('Thing' => '\Thing')      <- expected '\Vendor\Thing'
```

A class body with no file-level import at all still injects a bogus entry:

```php
'<?php namespace Acme; class Foo { use SomeTrait; }'
// array('SomeTrait' => '\SomeTrait')
```

End to end, the wrong entry reaches the generated document. `Reflector::getReturnType()`
resolves an unqualified docblock type through this map
(`getReturnTypesArray($returnType, $useStatements)`):

```
$ ./bin/phpdoc-md generate --bootstrap=boot.php 'Probe\Consumer'
| public | <strong>stamps()</strong> : <em>\Timestamps</em> |
```

for

```php
namespace Probe;
use Vendor\Timestamps;
class Consumer {
    use Timestamps;
    /** @return Timestamps */
    public function stamps() {}
}
```

The documented return type is `\Timestamps`, a class that does not exist. The correct
answer, `\Vendor\Timestamps`, was in the map until the class body overwrote it.

Trait conflict-resolution blocks corrupt the map further, because `readImport()` reads `{`
as the start of a group import:

```php
class C { use A, B { A::foo insteadof B; } }
// contributes 'A' => '\B\A'
```

## Impact

Every cross-reference derived from an unqualified docblock type in a class that uses a
trait points at a namespace that does not exist. The link is dead and the type is wrong,
and because the rendered text still looks like a fully qualified name nothing about the
output suggests a resolution failure.

## Fix

Track class-like bodies and skip any `use` inside one. In `getUseStatementsInString()`:

```php
$depth = 0;
$classBody = [];      // brace depths that are class/interface/trait/enum bodies
$pendingClass = false;

for ($i = 0, $len = count($tokens); $i < $len; $i++) {
    $token = $tokens[$i];

    if ($token === '{') {
        $depth++;
        if ($pendingClass) {
            $classBody[$depth] = true;
            $pendingClass = false;
        }
        continue;
    }
    if ($token === '}') {
        unset($classBody[$depth]);
        $depth--;
        continue;
    }
    if (!is_array($token)) {
        continue;
    }
    if (in_array($token[0], [T_CLASS, T_INTERFACE, T_TRAIT, T_ENUM], true)) {
        // "Foo::class" is also T_CLASS and opens no body.
        $pendingClass = $this->previousMeaningful($tokens, $i) !== '::';
        continue;
    }
    if ($token[0] !== T_USE || isset($classBody[$depth]) || $this->isClosureCapture($tokens, $i)) {
        continue;
    }
    foreach ($this->readImport($tokens, $i) as $localName => $import) {
        $usages[$localName] = Utils::sanitizeClassName($import);
    }
}
```

`previousMeaningful()` is the backward scan `isClosureCapture()` already performs — factor
that scan out and reuse it for both.

Add to `UseInspectorTest`: a source string that imports a trait at file level and uses it
in the class body, asserting the map still holds the fully qualified name; and one with a
trait `use` and no file-level import, asserting the map is empty.

## Resolution
getUseStatementsInString() tracks brace depth and marks class/interface/trait/enum bodies, skipping any use inside one; the closure-capture check was factored into previousMeaningfulText() and reused to exclude Foo::class. Four new UseInspectorTest cases including the trait-conflict block and a ::class constant.

## Problem

`CodeEntity` declares `$see` with a getter and a setter. `ClassEntity`, which extends it,
declares the same property again and overrides both accessors with the same bodies:

```php
// CodeEntity.php:20, 84-97
private array $see = [];

public function getSee(): array
{
    return $this->see;
}

public function setSee(array $see): self
{
    $this->see = [];
    foreach ($see as $i) {
        $this->see[] = $i;
    }

    return $this;
}
```

```php
// ClassEntity.php:26, 69-84
private array $see = [];

#[\Override]
public function getSee(): array
{
    return $this->see;
}

#[\Override]
public function setSee(array $see): self
{
    $this->see = [];
    foreach ($see as $i) {
        $this->see[] = $i;
    }

    return $this;
}
```

The two `setSee()` bodies are identical token for token. `FunctionEntity` does the same
thing a third time (FunctionEntity.php:23, 129-141), differing only in dropping the
re-index loop.

The property has to be redeclared *because* the accessors are overridden: `CodeEntity::$see`
is `private`, so the subclass copies cannot see it. Remove the overrides and the
redeclaration goes with them.

## Evidence

`ParamEntity` extends `CodeEntity` and overrides neither, using the base implementation
successfully. No caller depends on a subclass-specific `$see`: the only writer is
`DocInfoExtractor::applyInfoToEntity()`, which calls `$code->setSee(...)` through the
`CodeEntity` parameter type, and the only readers are
`PHPDocsMDCommand::execute()` (`$class->getSee()`) and `MDTableGenerator::addFunc()`
(`$func->getSee()`), neither of which chains off the setter's return value.

So all three copies are reached through the base class's declared interface, and two of
them exist only to shadow the first.

## Impact

Three private properties named `$see` and three accessor pairs implementing one rule. A
change to how `@see` entries are normalised has to be made in three places, and the
`#[\Override]` attributes make the duplication read as deliberate specialisation when
nothing is specialised.

## Fix

Widen the base return type so the subclasses lose nothing by inheriting, then delete the
copies:

```php
// CodeEntity.php
public function setSee(array $see): static
{
    $this->see = array_values($see);

    return $this;
}
```

Delete `$see`, `getSee()` and `setSee()` from `ClassEntity` and from `FunctionEntity`.

`: static` is what makes this safe: without it, `$classEntity->setSee([])` would start
returning the declared type `CodeEntity` and narrow the published signature. With it,
`ClassEntity::setSee()` still resolves to `ClassEntity` and `FunctionEntity::setSee()` to
`FunctionEntity`, exactly as the deleted overrides declared.

`array_values()` replaces the re-index loop that both surviving copies were performing by
hand. Verify with `vendor/bin/phpunit`, `vendor/bin/psalm` and `composer docs-check` —
`docs.md` will change, because two interface entries disappear from it, so regenerate it
with `composer docs` in the same commit.

## Resolution
CodeEntity::setSee() returns static and uses array_values(); the shadowing $see property and both accessors deleted from ClassEntity and FunctionEntity. Subclass return types are unchanged because of ': static'.

## Problem

`Reflector::__construct()` accepts four optional collaborators and defers construction of
each to a helper:

```php
public function __construct(
    string $className,
    ?FunctionFinder $functionFinder = null,
    ?DocInfoExtractor $docInfoExtractor = null,
    ?UseInspector $useInspector = null,
    ?ClassEntityFactory $classEntityFactory = null
) {
    $this->className = $className;
    $this->functionFinder = $this->loadIfNull($functionFinder, FunctionFinder::class);
    $this->docInfoExtractor = $this->loadIfNull($docInfoExtractor, DocInfoExtractor::class);
    $this->useInspector = $this->loadIfNull($useInspector, UseInspector::class);
    $this->classEntityFactory = $this->loadIfNull(
        $classEntityFactory,
        ClassEntityFactory::class,
        $this->docInfoExtractor
    );
}

private function loadIfNull(
    FunctionFinder|ClassEntityFactory|DocInfoExtractor|UseInspector|null $obj,
    string $className,
    ?object $in = null
): object {
    return is_object($obj) ? $obj : new $className($in);
}
```

Only `$functionFinder` is ever supplied. The last three parameters have never been passed a
value by any caller, first-party or test.

## Evidence

Every construction site in the repository:

```
$ grep -rn "new Reflector(" src test
src/PHPDocsMD/Console/PHPDocsMDCommand.php:485:  $reflector = new Reflector($name);
src/PHPDocsMD/FunctionFinder.php:64:            $reflector = new Reflector($className, $this);
test/ReflectorTest.php:41:                       new Reflector('Acme\\ExampleClassDepr');
test/ReflectorTest.php:47:                       new Reflector('Acme\\ExampleInterface');
...  (18 more, all one-argument)
```

Twenty-one call sites; one passes a second argument, none passes a third, fourth or fifth.
`FunctionFinder` passes `$this` because the reflector must reuse the finder's cache during
`{@inheritDoc}` resolution — that seam is real and load-bearing.

`loadIfNull()` exists only to service the three unused seams. Its signature has to
enumerate every collaborator type as a union and take an untyped `?object $in` to feed
`ClassEntityFactory`'s one constructor argument, which is why psalm reports
`InvalidStringClass` at Reflector.php:71.

This is the same defect class as `ReflectorInterface`, already removed for the same reason
(audit-e1287431): a substitution point nothing substitutes at.

## Impact

Dead flexibility on a published constructor. A reader has to determine which of the four
seams is real before touching the class, and `loadIfNull()`'s `new $className($in)` defeats
static analysis for all four — psalm cannot verify any of the four assignments, which is
the direct cause of the `InvalidStringClass` warning and part of why the class sits below
the project's inferred-types average.

## Fix

Keep the one seam that is used and construct the rest directly:

```php
public function __construct(string $className, ?FunctionFinder $functionFinder = null)
{
    $this->className = $className;
    $this->functionFinder = $functionFinder ?? new FunctionFinder();
    $this->docInfoExtractor = new DocInfoExtractor();
    $this->useInspector = new UseInspector();
    $this->classEntityFactory = new ClassEntityFactory($this->docInfoExtractor);
}
```

and delete `loadIfNull()`. Every assignment is then a concrete `new`, which psalm can check,
and the `InvalidStringClass` warning goes with it.

This narrows a public constructor signature, so it belongs in the same major release as the
`ReflectorInterface` removal. Verify with `vendor/bin/phpunit`, `vendor/bin/psalm` and
`composer docs-check`; `docs.md` changes because the constructor signature is documented,
so regenerate it with `composer docs`.

## Resolution
Constructor narrowed to (string $className, ?FunctionFinder $functionFinder = null); the three never-injected collaborators are constructed directly and loadIfNull() deleted. Psalm's InvalidStringClass warning went with it. BREAKING: narrows a public constructor signature.

## Problem

`Utils::sanitizeDeclaration()` splits a type declaration on `|` and treats each part as a
single type name:

```php
$parts = explode($delimiter, $typeDeclaration);
foreach ($parts as $key => $p) {
    if (self::shouldPrefixWithNamespace($p)) {
        $p = self::sanitizeClassName('\\' . trim($currentNameSpace, '\\') . '\\' . $p);
    } elseif (self::isClassReference($p)) {
        $p = self::sanitizeClassName($p);
    }
    $parts[$key] = $p;
}
```

`&` is not a delimiter it knows about. `isClassReference()` only rejects a declaration
containing a space, and an intersection written the way PHP writes it — `A&B` — has none,
so the whole intersection is taken for one unqualified class name and gets the current
namespace glued to the front.

`Reflector::formatReflectionType()` renders the *declared* intersection correctly, spaced
as `\Probe\A & \Probe\B`. The two then disagree and are concatenated.

## Evidence

```php
namespace Probe;

interface A {}
interface B {}

class Tags
{
    /** @param A&B $x an intersection */
    public function intersect(A&B $x) {}
}
```

```
$ ./bin/phpdoc-md generate --bootstrap=boot.php 'Probe\Tags'
| public | <strong>intersect(</strong><em>\Probe\A&B \| \Probe\A & \Probe\B</em> <strong>$x</strong>)</strong> : <em>void</em> |
```

`\Probe\A&B` is not a class. It is `A&B` with the namespace prefixed as though the
ampersand were part of an identifier. The correct rendering, `\Probe\A & \Probe\B`, is
produced too — from the signature — and sits beside it.

`sanitizeDeclaration()` already takes a `$delimiter` parameter for exactly this purpose:

```php
public static function sanitizeDeclaration(
    string $typeDeclaration,
    string $currentNameSpace,
    string $delimiter = '|'
): string
```

No caller has ever passed it.

## Impact

Every docblock-documented intersection type renders a class name that does not exist,
next to the correct one. Intersection types are PHP 8.1+ and uncommon today, which is why
this has gone unseen — but the project requires PHP 8.2 and up, so every supported version
can declare them.

## Fix

Use the delimiter parameter that is already there, recursing for the inner separator:

```php
public static function sanitizeDeclaration(
    string $typeDeclaration,
    string $currentNameSpace,
    string $delimiter = '|'
): string {
    $parts = explode($delimiter, $typeDeclaration);
    foreach ($parts as $key => $p) {
        // An intersection is not one class name — sanitize its members
        // individually, then let the union pass re-join them.
        if ($delimiter === '|' && str_contains($p, '&')) {
            $parts[$key] = self::sanitizeDeclaration($p, $currentNameSpace, '&');
            continue;
        }
        if (self::shouldPrefixWithNamespace($p)) {
            $p = self::sanitizeClassName('\\' . trim($currentNameSpace, '\\') . '\\' . $p);
        } elseif (self::isClassReference($p)) {
            $p = self::sanitizeClassName($p);
        }
        $parts[$key] = $p;
    }

    $parts = array_unique($parts, SORT_STRING);

    return implode($delimiter === '|' ? ' | ' : ' & ', $parts);
}
```

The recursion emits `\Probe\A & \Probe\B`, byte-identical to what
`formatReflectionType()` produces for the same signature, so the two sides then compare
equal and no concatenation happens at all.

